nodecode-ja-writing-guard is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-ja-writing-guard:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-ja-writing-guard
  commit ef2bc3c2c2c3162e9c281f3d359dfaaec629d6ad
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin was a rule catalog (rules.py), a detector with
masking, sentence analysis and scoring (detector.py), surface profiles
(surfaces.py), and a registration (__init__.py) that hooked
transform_llm_output to report every Japanese answer. The rewrite keeps the
catalog as data with the same rule ids, categories, labels, fix hints and
weights; the masking of code fences, inline code, URLs, quoted lines and
HTML; the 「」 skip; the sentence split and the rhythm, register and
list-ratio checks; the five surface profiles with their category and
rule scales, thresholds and aliases; the 0-100 saturation curve; the report's
Japanese summary and the finding order. The seam moved: the plugin's
post-processing hook is now one advice on NLE::EMIT-STREAM-PART, so the line
rides the answer itself in every shell, channel and transcript, and the
plugin's tool, slash command and help text are the cell's tool, command and
manual topic.

What was left out, and why: the opt-in enforce mode is not reproduced -- it
called the user's own model once or twice to rewrite an over-threshold answer
and checked that numbers, names, weekdays, URLs and code survived. A Nodecode
model can already rewrite what the tool reports, so the tool hands back the
score and the hints instead of spending two calls and a time budget behind the
answer. The post_tool_call observer that watched for MEDIA: attachment lines
also goes: enforce mode was the only thing that read it. The rule table is
trimmed to the catalog's most frequent rules (about seventy of the original's
ninety); the structural checks for uniform paragraph lengths and mixed-register
detection outside the surface rules are the original's and are kept.
